/// How likely something is to happen, as a named band rather than a number.
///
/// Here so a variant member can be an enum. The union's columns are every variant's members
/// side by side, and a row of another variant leaves this one blank - which is a different
/// path through the conversion than a blank `int`, and the one a real project hits.
enum Band
    /// Not said. A zero entry, which the Unreal target's `UENUM` requires of every enum - a
    /// default-constructed value has to name something.
    value None = 0
    /// Almost never.
    value Rare = 1
    /// About half the time.
    value Even = 2
    /// Nearly always.
    value Common = 3

/// Something a skill does to whoever it lands on.
///
/// Abstract: a row says which kind it is in its `$type` cell, and the columns of the group are
/// every variant's members side by side. spec/polymorphism.md section 5.
abstract struct Effect
    /// How likely it is to land, in percent. Every variant carries it, so it is one column and
    /// every row fills it.
    field chance int (min=0, max=100)

/// Takes health away.
struct DamageEffect extends Effect @1
    /// How much it takes.
    field damage int
    /// Whether it ignores armour.
    field pierces bool
    /// Which element it deals, as a row of that catalogue.
    ///
    /// **A reference on a variant member is the shape a real project reaches for first** - "the
    /// reward is an item, or a currency, or a monster" is that shape - and it is a different
    /// path twice over: the blank cells of the other variants go through the reference
    /// conversion, and the built variant has to carry the resolved row rather than the key.
    field elementId foreign Element

/// Gives health back.
struct HealEffect extends Effect @2
    /// How much it gives.
    field amount int
    /// How often it lands, as a band rather than a number.
    field band Band

/// Does nothing on its own and carries no members of its own.
///
/// Here because a variant with no fields has to work: what is left is the name, and the
/// discriminator is the whole of what the row carries. spec/polymorphism.md section 3.
struct NoEffect extends Effect @3

/// A variant that was dropped, kept as a tombstone so that `@4` is not handed to another one.
///
/// **This is what the notation is for and this fixture is where it is exercised end to end.**
/// A reader built while this variant existed still reads 4 as this shape, and rows written then
/// still carry it - so a new variant given 4 would be read as this one, with no error anywhere.
/// Nothing is generated for it: `PolymorphicRecordTests` asserts the name appears in no output
/// file, which is what says a tombstone declares nothing. spec/polymorphism.md section 5.1.1.
struct BurnEffect extends Effect @4 (removed)
    /// Kept below the line it was dropped on, which is the point of the notation: `(removed)`
    /// goes on the declaration and nothing else in the file moves.
    field burnTurns int
